<%# Career, on CAREER.md's asymmetry argument. The framing rule from that
    doc matters and is honoured throughout: this is not a
    white-collar-ladder optimiser, and every claim has to hold for a shift
    lead as much as for a senior engineer. %>

<section class="mt-12 max-w-3xl space-y-5">
  <p>
    Your employer negotiates pay every week. They know the band, the market
    and what your alternatives are worth. You negotiate once every few
    years, from a position of knowing almost none of that, usually while
    anxious and under time pressure.
  </p>
  <p>
    That asymmetry is most of why wages go the way they go, and it is worth
    real money to close even slightly. A raise won at twenty-eight is paid
    every year afterwards. An interview you walked into unprepared costs the
    whole offer. This section exists for those moments.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="mt-14">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">What you see</h2>
  <div class="mt-6 grid grid-cols-1 gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "Your wins, logged as they happen") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        The thing you fixed, the shift you covered, the number you moved —
        captured in a sentence at the time, when you remember it. Two years
        later that list is the raise case, and it's the difference between
        "I work hard" and six specific things with dates on them.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "What you're paid, over time") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        Every change recorded, so tenure and trajectory are facts you have
        rather than things you half-remember. Also the input the pay-stub
        checker uses to notice when a number moved and nobody told you.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "The search, paced") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        Job hunting collapses into either frantic or nothing. Here it's a
        plan with an order to it — profile, then materials, then a steady
        cadence of applications — with each step small enough to actually
        do after work.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "Listings you're watching") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        Followed in the app rather than in a company's feed, so the search
        doesn't require logging into a platform that's optimising for
        something other than your outcome.
      </p>
    <% end %>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="mt-14 max-w-3xl">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">Not a ladder optimiser</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    Every tool here has to make sense for a shift lead at a distribution
    centre as much as for a salaried professional. That means no assumed
    degree, no assumed spotless history, no assumed profile on a networking
    site, and no output written in the dialect of people who already have
    all three. Unpaid wages and a first formal accommodation request sit
    beside interview practice, because that is what the range of working
    life actually looks like.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="mt-14 max-w-3xl">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">It works for you, not your employer</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    There's a version of career software that quietly starts thinking like
    the company — how to make yourself a more attractive asset, how to be
    easier to manage. This isn't that. The posture throughout is the
    negotiation one: even the odds, get the most for your time, and say
    plainly when the fact you'd need has to come from outside rather than
    inventing a market number that sounds authoritative.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="mt-14 max-w-3xl">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">When it goes wrong</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    Losing a job touches everything at once — income, insurance, bills, the
    search, and your ability to think straight for a fortnight. Because
    this app holds all of those already, that becomes one plan across all of
    them rather than five separate crises you have to sequence yourself
    while shaken.
  </p>
</section>
